  card="As hunter-gatherers gained familiarity with seasons and different types of food resources, there was a gradual transition to a food-producing way of life, known as the Neolithic revolution. Its hallmark was the domestication of select animals and plants, bringing them under human control, and the development of new breeds through cultivation and husbandry. While hunter-gatherers made tools for procuring food, Neolithic farmers made tools for food production and processing and developed earthenware pottery in many shapes and sizes. They established the first village settlements, laying the foundations for the urban revolution. The transition did not occur at the same time everywhere but at different periods in different areas, such as wheat and barley in West Asia, millets and rice in India and China.",
